; mini.mx -- one grammar, two languages out, and the target named from outside.
;
; The header is two `@use` lines and no rules of its own: lib/mini.mx is the
; grammar of the little language below, lib/mini-c.mx says what its eleven
; constructs are written as in C, and lib/mini-python.mx, which this file does
; not use, says what they are written as in Python. Reading this file gives the
; C beside it in examples/mini.out.
;
; The same body with the other backend is the point of the pair, and it needs
; the two-file form, because a file that carries its own body carries its own
; header with it:
;
;     mx -u lib/mini.mx -u lib/mini-python.mx -i prog.mini
;
; tests/mini.sh does exactly that: it takes the body below as a file of its
; own, reads it out to C and to Python, compiles the one, runs the other, and
; requires the two programs to print the same three numbers. Two targets that
; agree on a wrong answer would be caught by the numbers being pinned there as
; well.

@use "../lib/mini.mx"
@use "../lib/mini-c.mx"
@end
program {
    let total = 0;
    let i = 1;
    while i < 6 do {
        let sq = i * i;
        set total = total + sq;
        if 9 < sq then { print sq };
        set i = i + 1
    };
    print total
}
